  <h1>About nursinghomeneglectcalc.com</h1>

  <p>nursinghomeneglectcalc.com is a free elder law resource maintained by The Click Lab. Our damages calculator and guides help families who suspect nursing home neglect or abuse understand what a realistic recovery range looks like, what legal theories apply, and what to do in the critical days after discovering harm to a loved one &mdash; before they've spoken with an attorney. The Site is built for people who are often managing a care crisis and their own grief at the same time, and who want a plain-English starting point, not a substitute for legal or medical advice.</p>

  <h2>What we do</h2>
  <p>Nursing home neglect and abuse claims sit at the intersection of negligence law, state elder abuse statutes, and federal nursing home regulation &mdash; a combination most families have never had to navigate before. We translate that complexity into plain-English calculators and guides, reviewed by our <a href="/editorial/">editorial team</a> with citations to the Nursing Home Reform Act, CMS regulations, and state elder abuse statutes. The calculator estimates a damages range based on the type of harm (pressure sores, falls, malnutrition, medication errors, abuse, or wrongful death), medical costs, whether an enhanced-remedy elder abuse statute applies, and whether the conduct was negligent or intentional &mdash; the factors that most directly move a real nursing home neglect recovery.</p>

  <h2>Who this calculator is for</h2>
  <p>Most visitors fall into one of a few groups: family members who've just discovered a pressure sore, an unexplained fall, or signs of malnutrition and want to understand roughly what's at stake before hiring an attorney; families further along who are trying to make sense of CMS deficiency citations or an arbitration clause in the admission agreement; and paralegals or intake staff who want a quick reference point before pulling the specific state statute and facility history that apply to a given case. We built the Site around these use cases, which is why every guide leads with a plain-English answer before it gets into the regulatory or litigation detail.</p>

  <h2>How the estimate is built</h2>
  <p>The full mechanics &mdash; the pain-and-suffering baselines by harm type, the elder abuse statute enhancement, the punitive damages estimate, and what the calculator doesn't model &mdash; are documented on our <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a>. At a high level: the calculator starts from a documented baseline for the type of harm involved, adjusts upward when a state elder abuse statute with enhanced remedies applies, and adds a punitive component when the conduct was intentional or reckless rather than merely negligent. Pulling the facility's actual CMS inspection history, evaluating arbitration clause enforceability, and confirming which family members can bring a claim in your state is work that requires an elder abuse attorney who has reviewed the facility's records.</p>

  <h2>What we don't do</h2>
  <p>We don't provide legal advice, and nothing on this Site creates an attorney-client relationship. Nursing home neglect recoveries depend heavily on the facility's documented history of deficiency citations, the strength of the medical and care-plan records, the applicable state's elder abuse statute, and whether an arbitration clause is enforced &mdash; variables no calculator can fully account for. We also don't evaluate individual cases, review specific settlement offers, or file claims or reports on anyone's behalf. For your specific situation, consult a licensed elder abuse or nursing home neglect attorney; the case-review form on our homepage is a disclosed way to connect with one, and see our <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> for more on finding one.</p>

  <h2>Editorial standards</h2>
  <p>Every guide and reference page is written from primary sources &mdash; the Nursing Home Reform Act, CMS regulations and published deficiency data, state elder abuse statutes, and published verdict and settlement data &mdash; and reviewed before publication. We don't publish thin content: each page is meant to answer a specific question a family would actually ask in the days after discovering potential neglect, with enough detail to be useful during a genuinely difficult moment. Our full editorial workflow, contributor backgrounds, and sourcing policy are on the <a href="/editorial/">editorial team page</a>.</p>

  <h2>How we make money</h2>
  <p>Display advertising via Google AdSense. We don't accept paid placements in our editorial content, and no law firm, nursing home operator, or long-term care insurer pays for a favorable mention in our guides or reference pages. Separately, the case-review form on our homepage is a disclosed advertising product: if you submit it, your name, phone, email, and case details are shared with attorneys who may compensate The Click Lab Agency LLC for the referral. That's optional, and separate from the editorial content on this Site. See our <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> for exactly what's shared if you use that form.</p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>Questions, corrections, or feedback: see our <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.</p>
